WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:09.487
Imagine two buttons and a puck. The blue button sends the puck left; the yellow button sends it right. Watch what happens when I press blue.

00:00:09.907 --> 00:00:18.110
Now hide the buttons. Just by looking at the puck, you can tell which one I pressed. My choice has left a readable mark on the world.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:27.660
Let's change one thing. Disconnect both buttons, and let a fan push the puck randomly left or right. There are still two possible outcomes.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:36.816
But now seeing the puck tells you nothing about my choice. Variety by itself is not control. We need outcomes that depend on the input.

00:00:37.566 --> 00:00:46.783
Put those experiments side by side. Both produce the same overall spread of puck positions. Only the working buttons let us send a message.

00:00:47.203 --> 00:00:58.765
Mutual information measures how much of our choice survives in the outcome. With two equally likely, perfectly readable buttons, that is one bit, or about point six nine nats.

00:01:00.965 --> 00:01:08.590
A skill is a button that runs a whole behavior. Give the robot a blue label and keep that label fixed while it moves.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:17.159
Give it yellow, and it can learn a different behavior. The policy sees both its current state and the chosen label at every step.

00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:27.615
Now collect many runs. Blue leaves a cloud of visited states; yellow leaves another. Forget the individual paths for a moment and look at those clouds.

00:01:28.035 --> 00:01:36.427
Where the clouds overlap, the label is hard to guess. As they separate, a state becomes stronger evidence about which skill was running.

00:01:37.177 --> 00:01:47.547
This is the central idea of DIAYN, pronounced dye in: learn skills whose states make their identities recognizable, before specifying a task reward.

00:01:47.967 --> 00:01:58.537
The method also encourages action randomness within each skill. That flexibility is useful, provided the different skills still leave distinguishable patterns of states.

00:02:00.737 --> 00:02:11.095
Suppose we have four skill labels, all equally likely. Before seeing the robot, a guesser assigns each label probability one quarter. This is the prior.

00:02:11.515 --> 00:02:21.723
After seeing a state, the guesser updates its probabilities. The probability assigned to the skill that actually ran tells us how much evidence the state provides.

00:02:22.473 --> 00:02:31.881
Compare the probability after seeing the state with the probability before. Divide the two, then take a logarithm. That gives the intrinsic reward.

00:02:32.301 --> 00:02:42.371
Why a ratio? A guess of one quarter was already available without observing anything. We reward the extra evidence created by the robot's behavior.

00:02:43.121 --> 00:02:52.912
A perfect guesser would recover mutual information when we average this reward. In practice, we train a neural classifier, so its guesses can be wrong.

00:02:53.332 --> 00:03:03.539
The resulting average is a lower bound. The missing amount is a nonnegative divergence between the true posterior and our classifier, averaged over states.

00:03:05.739 --> 00:03:15.003
Let's put numbers on the picture. The true label is blue. Its prior probability is one quarter, and the classifier now assigns it point eight.

00:03:15.423 --> 00:03:25.043
That is three point two times the prior probability. Taking the natural logarithm gives a positive reward of about one point one six nats.

00:03:25.793 --> 00:03:34.237
Now move the state into the overlap. The classifier returns to one quarter for blue, exactly where it started before seeing the state.

00:03:34.657 --> 00:03:44.683
The ratio becomes one, and its logarithm becomes zero. A state that reveals nothing about the label earns no information reward in this example.

00:03:45.433 --> 00:03:56.376
Finally, imagine a confident mistake. The classifier assigns the true blue label probability point zero five, even though it strongly prefers another label.

00:03:56.796 --> 00:04:06.824
Blue's reward is now negative. Confidence alone is not the target; the observation has to provide evidence for the skill that was actually selected.

00:04:09.024 --> 00:04:17.712
Where do the training labels come from? We chose them ourselves. Sample blue, run the policy, and store the visited states with that blue label.

00:04:18.132 --> 00:04:28.021
These labeled examples train the classifier. It learns which regions of the world each skill tends to visit, without receiving a downstream task reward.

00:04:28.771 --> 00:04:38.434
Then the classifier supplies rewards to the policy. States that make blue easier to recognize become attractive when the robot is running the blue skill.

00:04:38.854 --> 00:04:49.606
The policy improves, its state cloud changes, and the classifier learns again. Picture a feedback loop between better experiments and a better reader of their effects.

00:04:50.356 --> 00:05:01.000
At first the clouds may nearly coincide. Learning can pull them apart, while the action entropy term allows multiple ways of producing each recognizable pattern.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:11.273
This is an optimization process, not a guarantee. A weak classifier can give poor rewards, and the features it sees determine which differences count.

00:05:13.473 --> 00:05:23.094
After training, the labels act like a small menu of behaviors. A later task can try them, select one, or build a longer sequence from several.

00:05:23.514 --> 00:05:33.614
But the menu need not contain every useful behavior. Learning distinguishable skills does not prove that the robot can solve an arbitrary future task.

00:05:34.364 --> 00:05:44.102
Return to our buttons, now placed at a junction. Each button takes the robot down a different one-way passage. The choices are perfectly distinguishable.

00:05:44.522 --> 00:05:54.527
Yet after leaving the junction, the robot may have very few choices left. Demonstrating control and preserving future control are different objectives.

00:05:55.277 --> 00:06:05.762
Keep the picture of the two clouds in mind. DIAYN asks for behaviors whose states reveal which skill produced them. The classifier turns that question into reward.

00:06:06.182 --> 00:06:16.308
Next, we will ask where a robot should stand so that many such choices remain available. That takes us from learning skills to the geometry of empowerment.
